{% if 'oauth-correctness' in flags %}
OAUTH 2.0 / RFC 8628 CORRECTNESS (Device Authorization Grant)
- Authorization request must produce a device_code + user_code + verification_uri
- Polling endpoint must return authorization_pending until approved, then mint tokens
- Token grant must bind to the user who approved, not a placeholder
- Device codes must expire and be single-use
- Refresh token rotation should invalidate the previous refresh token
- Bearer tokens must be cryptographically verifiable (signature) AND revocable
{% endif %}

{% if 'jwt' in flags %}
JWT CLAIM DESIGN
- Required claims present (sub, email, organization_*, workspace_*)
- exp/iat set and sensible
- iss/aud fields consistent
- No PII leakage in claims
- Claims match what downstream consumers (CLI status decoders, etc.) read
{% endif %}

{% if 'async-patterns' in flags %}
ASYNC PATTERNS
- No sync DB I/O inside async handlers (missing greenlet risk)
- No lazy relationship access on async sessions (user.org without await)
- No blocking calls (time.sleep, requests) inside async fns
- Proper await db.commit() before returning
- httpx (not requests) for outbound HTTP from async code
{% endif %}

{% if 'security' in flags %}
SECURITY
- Password hashing uses memory-hard algorithm (Argon2id, NOT sha256/pbkdf2)
- No secrets in plaintext, logs, or git history
- Authorization checks before resource access
- Rate limiting on sensitive endpoints
- CORS, CSP, CSRF posture where relevant
{% endif %}

